<h1>Work, Energy and Power</h1>

<p>Work, energy, and power are three of the most important and widely used concepts in physics. They connect Newton’s laws to practical questions: How much effort does it take to lift a weight? How fast can an engine do useful work? How is energy stored and transferred? These ideas underpin the design of everything from cars and hydroelectric dams to the human body’s metabolism.</p>

<h2>Part 1: Work</h2>

<h3>Definition of Work</h3>
<p>In everyday language, "work" means any effort. In physics, the definition is more precise: <strong>work is done when a force causes a displacement of the object on which it acts</strong>.</p>

<div class="formula">
  W = F · d · cos θ
</div>

<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>W</strong> = Work done (Joules, J)</li>
  <li><strong>F</strong> = Magnitude of the applied force (Newtons, N)</li>
  <li><strong>d</strong> = Displacement of the object (metres, m)</li>
  <li><strong>θ</strong> = Angle between the force vector and the displacement vector</li>
</ul>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Important:</strong> If the force is perpendicular to the displacement (θ = 90°), then cos 90° = 0 and <em>no work is done</em>. A waiter holding a tray steady while walking horizontally does zero work on the tray in the physics sense, because the upward force is perpendicular to the horizontal displacement.
</div>

<h3>Special Cases</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Situation</th><th>θ</th><th>cos θ</th><th>Work Done</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Force parallel to displacement (pushing forward)</td><td>0°</td><td>1</td><td>W = Fd (maximum, positive)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Force at 60° to displacement</td><td>60°</td><td>0.5</td><td>W = 0.5Fd (positive)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Force perpendicular to displacement</td><td>90°</td><td>0</td><td>W = 0 (no work)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Force opposing displacement (friction)</td><td>180°</td><td>−1</td><td>W = −Fd (negative work)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>
<div class="worked">
  <h4>Problem</h4>
  <p>A person pushes a 20 kg box with a force of 60 N at an angle of 0° (horizontal) for a distance of 5 m. How much work is done?</p>
  <h4>Solution</h4>
  <p>W = F × d × cos 0° = 60 × 5 × 1 = <strong>300 J</strong></p>
</div>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>
<div class="worked">
  <h4>Problem</h4>
  <p>A student pulls a suitcase with a force of 40 N at 30° above the horizontal for 10 m. How much work is done by the pulling force?</p>
  <h4>Solution</h4>
  <p>W = 40 × 10 × cos 30° = 400 × 0.866 = <strong>346 J</strong></p>
</div>

<h3>Unit of Work</h3>
<p>The SI unit of work is the <strong>Joule (J)</strong>. 1 J = 1 N·m = 1 kg·m²/s².</p>

<h2>Part 2: Kinetic Energy</h2>

<p><strong>Kinetic energy (KE)</strong> is the energy an object possesses due to its motion.</p>

<div class="formula">
  KE = ½ m v²
</div>

<p>Where <strong>m</strong> is mass (kg) and <strong>v</strong> is speed (m/s). The unit is Joules.</p>

<h3>The Work-Energy Theorem</h3>
<p>The net work done on an object equals the change in its kinetic energy:</p>
<div class="formula">
  W<sub>net</sub> = ΔKE = KE<sub>final</sub> − KE<sub>initial</sub> = ½mv<sub>f</sub>² − ½mv<sub>i</sub>²
</div>

<div class="worked">
  <h4>Example: Braking Car</h4>
  <p>A 1000 kg car travelling at 20 m/s brakes to a stop. How much work is done by the brakes?</p>
  <p>W = ΔKE = ½(1000)(0²) − ½(1000)(20²) = 0 − 200,000 = <strong>−200,000 J = −200 kJ</strong></p>
  <p>The negative sign indicates the braking force acts opposite to displacement, removing energy from the car.</p>
</div>

<h2>Part 3: Potential Energy</h2>

<p><strong>Potential energy</strong> is stored energy that an object has due to its position or configuration.</p>

<h3>Gravitational Potential Energy</h3>
<div class="formula">
  GPE = m g h
</div>
<p>Where <strong>h</strong> is the height above a chosen reference level (usually the ground), g = 9.8 m/s².</p>

<div class="worked">
  <h4>Example</h4>
  <p>A 5 kg book is lifted to a shelf 2 m above the floor. What is its gravitational potential energy relative to the floor?</p>
  <p>GPE = 5 × 9.8 × 2 = <strong>98 J</strong></p>
</div>

<h3>Elastic Potential Energy</h3>
<p>Energy stored in a compressed or stretched spring: PE<sub>elastic</sub> = ½kx², where k is the spring constant (N/m) and x is the extension or compression.</p>

<h2>Part 4: Conservation of Energy</h2>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Law of Conservation of Energy:</strong> Energy cannot be created or destroyed — it can only be transferred from one form to another. The total mechanical energy (KE + PE) of an isolated system (with no friction) remains constant.
</div>

<div class="formula">
  KE<sub>initial</sub> + PE<sub>initial</sub> = KE<sub>final</sub> + PE<sub>final</sub>
</div>

<h3>Example: Ball Dropped from a Height</h3>
<div class="worked">
  <h4>Problem</h4>
  <p>A 2 kg ball is dropped from a height of 5 m. What is its speed just before it hits the ground? (ignore air resistance)</p>
  <h4>Solution</h4>
  <p>At top: KE = 0, PE = mgh = 2 × 9.8 × 5 = 98 J</p>
  <p>At bottom: PE = 0, KE = 98 J</p>
  <p>½mv² = 98 → v² = 98 × 2/2 = 98 → v = √98 ≈ <strong>9.9 m/s</strong></p>
</div>

<h3>Real World: Energy with Friction</h3>
<p>When friction is present, some mechanical energy is converted to heat (thermal energy). The total energy is still conserved, but the mechanical energy decreases:</p>
<div class="formula">
  KE<sub>i</sub> + PE<sub>i</sub> = KE<sub>f</sub> + PE<sub>f</sub> + W<sub>friction</sub>
</div>
<p>W<sub>friction</sub> is the magnitude of the work done against friction (energy lost to heat).</p>

<h2>Part 5: Power</h2>

<p><strong>Power</strong> is the rate at which work is done (or energy is transferred).</p>

<div class="formula">
  P = W / t
</div>

<p>Where <strong>P</strong> is power (Watts, W), <strong>W</strong> is work done (J), and <strong>t</strong> is time (s). 1 Watt = 1 J/s.</p>

<p>Power can also be expressed in terms of force and velocity:</p>
<div class="formula">
  P = F × v (when force and velocity are parallel)
</div>

<h3>Worked Example: Climbing Stairs</h3>
<div class="worked">
  <h4>Problem</h4>
  <p>A 60 kg student climbs a staircase 4 m high in 8 seconds. What is her average power output?</p>
  <h4>Solution</h4>
  <p>Work done = mgh = 60 × 9.8 × 4 = 2352 J</p>
  <p>P = W/t = 2352/8 = <strong>294 W</strong></p>
</div>

<h3>Common Power Units</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Unit</th><th>Equivalent</th><th>Common Use</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Watt (W)</td><td>1 J/s</td><td>Electrical appliances, light bulbs</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Kilowatt (kW)</td><td>1000 W</td><td>Car engines, large appliances</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Megawatt (MW)</td><td>1,000,000 W</td><td>Power stations</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Horsepower (hp)</td><td>≈ 746 W</td><td>Car engines (older units)</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Part 6: Efficiency</h2>

<p><strong>Efficiency</strong> measures how well a machine or process converts input energy into useful output energy. No real machine is 100% efficient — some energy is always lost to heat, sound, or vibration.</p>

<div class="formula">
  Efficiency (%) = (Useful Output Energy / Total Input Energy) × 100%
</div>

<div class="worked">
  <h4>Example: Electric Motor</h4>
  <p>An electric motor uses 500 J of electrical energy to do 350 J of useful mechanical work. What is its efficiency?</p>
  <p>Efficiency = (350/500) × 100% = <strong>70%</strong></p>
  <p>The remaining 30% (150 J) was lost as heat.</p>
</div>

<h2>Summary Table</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Formula</th><th>SI Unit</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Work</td><td>W = Fd cos θ</td><td>Joule (J)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Kinetic Energy</td><td>KE = ½mv²</td><td>Joule (J)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gravitational PE</td><td>GPE = mgh</td><td>Joule (J)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conservation of Energy</td><td>KE<sub>i</sub> + PE<sub>i</sub> = KE<sub>f</sub> + PE<sub>f</sub></td><td>—</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Power</td><td>P = W/t = Fv</td><td>Watt (W)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Efficiency</td><td>η = (Useful output / Total input) × 100%</td><td>% (dimensionless)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> Work and energy are closely linked — work is the mechanism by which energy is transferred. Energy takes many forms (kinetic, potential, thermal, electrical) but the total amount is always conserved. Power tells you <em>how fast</em> that energy transfer happens.
</div>
<div id="live-file-panel" style="display:none;margin:20px 0;padding:16px;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:6px;background:#f9f9f9;">
  <strong>Live File</strong>
  <div id="live-file-content" style="margin-top:8px;color:#555;"></div>
</div>
<script>
(function(){
  const parts = window.location.pathname.split('/').filter(Boolean);
  const apiBase = 'http://172.31.184.39:8000';
  let course = '', week = '';
  if(parts.length >= 3 && parts[0] === 'courses'){
    course = parts[1];
    week = parts[2];
  } else if(parts.length >= 2 && parts[0] !== 'courses'){
    course = parts[0];
    week = parts[1];
  } else {
    return;
  }
  course = course.trim();
  week = week.trim();
  if(!course || !week) return;
  const panel = document.getElementById('live-file-panel');
  const content = document.getElementById('live-file-content');
    function renderFile(meta){
    if(!meta){
      panel.style.display = 'none';
      return;
    }
    const url = apiBase + '/live-file/' + course + '/' + week;
    const dlUrl = url + '?download=1';
    const type = meta.content_type || '';
    const safeName = meta.filename || 'file';
    let html = '<div><a href="' + dlUrl + '" style="color:#2572F5;text-decoration:underline;cursor:pointer;">Download ' + safeName + '</a></div>';
    if(type.startsWith('image/')){
      fetch(url).then(r=>r.blob()).then(b=>{
        const el = document.getElementById('live-file-img');
        if(el) el.src = URL.createObjectURL(b);
      });
      html += '<div style="margin-top:10px;"><img id="live-file-img" src="" alt="' + safeName + '" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:4px;"/></div>';
    } else if(type === 'application/pdf'){
      fetch(url).then(r=>r.blob()).then(b=>{
        const el = document.getElementById('live-file-iframe');
        if(el) el.src = URL.createObjectURL(b);
      });
      html += '<div style="margin-top:10px;"><iframe id="live-file-iframe" src="" style="width:100%;height:480px;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:4px;"></iframe></div>';
    } else if(type.startsWith('text/')){
      fetch(url).then(r=>r.blob()).then(b=>{
        const el = document.getElementById('live-file-iframe');
        if(el) el.src = URL.createObjectURL(b);
      });
      html += '<div style="margin-top:10px;"><iframe id="live-file-iframe" src="" style="width:100%;height:320px;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:4px;background:#fff;"></iframe></div>';
    }
    content.innerHTML = html;
    panel.style.display = 'block';
  }
  async function load(){
    try{
      const r = await fetch(apiBase + '/admin/live-file/' + course + '/' + week);
      if(!r.ok){renderFile(null);return;}
      const d = await r.json();
      renderFile(d || null);
    }catch{
      renderFile(null);
    }
  }
  load();
  setInterval(load, 15000);
})();
</script>
